        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "T. Pfeiffer",
                "firstName": "T.",
                "lastName": "Pfeiffer"
            },
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "D. Dudek",
                "firstName": "D.",
                "lastName": "Dudek"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "To create the 'missing link' between thick and thin film in terms of dry processing and resolution capabilities a new metallisation technology has been developed based on photo-tackification and toning technique. This additive process is named PCS for photoformed conductors on ceramic substrates. The PCS process consists of coating a thin, amorphous layer of a mixture of nitrophenyl dihydropyridine derivatives onto a ceramic substrate, exposing it through a mask to UV light and thereby transferring it into a tacky state. The exposed ceramic undergoes a toning process with appropriate metal toner materials thus forming the circuit pattern with photographic accuracy. Finally, conventional thick film firing leads to the finished metal circuit.<<ETX xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">&gt;</ETX>",
        "keywords": {
